// Introduction.
//
// What WCL is and why it exists. The hands-on walkthrough (install, first
// document, first schema, first wdoc site) lives in quickstart.wcl; the two
// were one chapter until they were split. Written against #158.
page intro {
  // Served at the book's root (`/reference/`).
  start = true

  title = "Introduction"

  h1 "Introduction"

  p "WCL is a typed configuration and schema language. A `.wcl` file carries the data: the ports, the replica counts, the URLs. It also carries the shape that data has to fit. One command, `wcl check`, holds the shape against the data, and it does so before the program that needs the configuration has started."

  p "This chapter is about why that matters and what it buys you. It sets WCL against the formats you already use, and it introduces **wdoc**, the document generator built on the same language. If you would rather start typing, [Quick start](quickstart) installs the tool and walks a first document from four lines to a checked schema."

  h2 "What WCL is for"

  p "Configuration is the part of a system with no compiler. The code around it is type-checked, reviewed and tested. The file that decides which database it talks to is a bag of strings that nothing looks at until the process boots. So a missing key is a crash at 3am, a `replicas: 600` is an outage, and a `debug: true` that outlived its pull request is an incident report."

  p "The usual answer is to describe the shape somewhere else: a JSON Schema file, a Kubernetes CRD, a `struct` with `#[serde(deny_unknown_fields)]` in the program that reads it. That works, and it puts the description of the data in a different file, a different language, and often a different repository from the data itself. The two drift."

  p "WCL's answer is that the shape belongs in the language. A `.wcl` file declares its own types, and `wcl check` holds the data against them. It is an ordinary command, so you can run it in CI, in a pre-commit hook, or on your laptop before you push. Nothing has to consume the file for `wcl check` to judge it."

  p "The type system carries more than the strings, numbers and booleans of a data format:"

  list {
    li "**Sized numbers and units.** `u32`, `f64`, and literals like `512MiB` and `90s` that evaluate to a plain number in a declared base unit. See [Values and primitives](lang_values)."
    li "**Names that mean something.** `identifier` for a name the rest of the document may point at, `symbol` for a tag, and a `symbol_set` for a closed vocabulary whose members are checked."
    li "**Composite types.** Records, lists, tensors, unions, interfaces and type aliases with `@min` / `@max` / `@non_empty` constraints. See [Types](lang_types)."
    li "**Expressions and functions.** The right of an `=` is an expression, not just a literal: arithmetic, string interpolation, conditionals, `let` bindings, calls. See [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions) and [Functions](lang_functions)."
    li "**A document model.** Blocks nest, a schema decides what may nest inside what, and gather fields collect every block of a kind into a list the host can walk. See [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents)."
  }

  h2 "How WCL differs from JSON, YAML and TOML"

  p "Take a real fragment. Here is a service deployed to two environments, written the way most projects write it today:"

  code yaml {
    filename = "app.yaml"
    source = <<'YAML'
service: checkout

environments:
  staging:
    url: https://staging.example.com
    replicas: 1
    debug: yes
  prod:
    url: https://example.com
    replicas: 6
    debug: no
YAML
  }

  p "Nothing in that file says `replicas` holds a number. Nothing says 600 would be absurd. Nothing says `environments` may hold `staging` and `prod` and not `stagng`. Every one of those is the consuming program's problem, discovered at startup, in production, by whoever is on call. And `debug: yes` is not even reliably a boolean: YAML 1.1 parsers read `yes` and `no` as `true` and `false`, YAML 1.2 parsers read them as the strings `\"yes\"` and `\"no\"`. Which one you get depends on a library you did not choose."

  p "The same data in WCL, with the shape written down beside it:"

  code wcl {
    filename = "app.wcl"
    source = <<'WCL'
@block("env")
type Env {
  @inline(0) name: identifier
  url:      utf8
  replicas: u32
  debug:    bool
}

@document
type AppConfig {
  service: utf8
  @children("env") envs: list<Env>
}

service = "checkout"

env staging {
  url      = "https://staging.example.com"
  replicas = 1
  debug    = true
}

env prod {
  url      = "https://example.com"
  replicas = 6
  debug    = false
}
WCL
  }

  p "It is longer, and the extra length is the whole point: the top half is the contract, and it is the reason `wcl check` can answer a question about the bottom half. There is no `debug: yes` ambiguity to resolve, because `yes` is not a boolean in WCL. It is a name, and a name that nothing declares fails to resolve."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Question"              | "JSON"                        | "YAML"                                  | "TOML"                        | "WCL" |
      | "Comments"              | "No"                          | "Yes"                                   | "Yes"                         | "Yes, and `wcl fmt` keeps them" |
      | "Scalar types"          | "String, number, bool, null"  | "The same, guessed from the text"       | "Plus dates and times"        | "Sized integers, floats, units, `bool`, four string encodings, identifiers, symbols, `none`" |
      | "Where the schema lives"| "Another file (JSON Schema)"  | "Another file"                          | "Another file"                | "This file, or one it imports" |
      | "Computed values"       | "No"                          | "Anchors and aliases reuse, never compute" | "No"                       | "Full expressions, `let` bindings and functions" |
      | "Who validates"         | "The consuming program"       | "The consuming program"                 | "The consuming program"       | "`wcl check`, before anything consumes it" |
      | "Reusing a fragment"    | "Copy it"                     | "`&anchor` / `*alias`"                  | "Copy it"                     | "`import`, `let`, functions, references" |
  }

  p "The last row is the one that compounds. A configuration format without composition grows by copying, and a file that grew by copying is a file where two of the copies disagree. [Namespaces and imports](lang_namespaces) covers how to split a document across files. [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions) covers how to compute one field from another."

  callout "WCL is not a general-purpose language" {
    class = ["note"]
    body  = "There is no I/O, no mutation, no loops that can fail to terminate. Every expression evaluates to a value, and evaluation is lazy and cached. See [How a document evaluates](lang_evaluation). What WCL adds over a data format is types, composition and checking, not a runtime."
  }

  h2 "The other half: wdoc"

  p "**wdoc** is a document generator written in WCL. You declare pages and sites as blocks, and `wcl wdoc` renders them to a static website, a Markdown tree or a PDF. The book you are reading is a wdoc site. It is not a second language: `import <wdoc.wcl>` pulls in a library of type declarations shipped inside the binary, so every block it gives you is an ordinary `@block` type of the kind you can declare yourself. The same checker reads it, and the same errors come back."

  p "[Introduction to wdoc](wdoc_intro) is the full account, and it opens Part II. [Quick start](quickstart#your-first-wdoc-site) builds a site in a dozen lines."

  h2 "How to read this book"

  p "This is one reference for both halves, in three parts."

  table {
    rows:
      | "Part"                | "What it covers" |
      | "Front matter"        | "This chapter and [Quick start](quickstart): what WCL is for and how it differs from a data format, then the shortest path from an empty folder to a checked document." |
      | "[The WCL language](lang_documents)" | "Documents, values, collections, types, expressions, control flow, functions, namespaces, schemas, decorators, connections, evaluation, builtins, and the CLI." |
      | "[wdoc](wdoc_intro)"  | "Its own [introduction](wdoc_intro), then pages and sites, templates, themes, visibility and output targets. Then the block reference: text, code, callouts, tables, media, icons, math, data views, diagrams, charts, timelines, terminals, wireframes, and writing your own." |
  }

  p "Read Part I in order the first time. Its first five chapters build on one another: [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents) is the shape of a file, [Values and primitives](lang_values) is what goes in it, [Lists, tensors and records](lang_collections) is what those compose into, [Types](lang_types) is how you name a shape, and [Expressions and operators](lang_expressions) is how you compute one. After those five the chapters are largely independent, and [Schemas](lang_schemas) is the one to reach for when a `wcl check` message surprises you."

  p "Part II assumes Part I and otherwise stands alone. Read [Introduction to wdoc](wdoc_intro) for what it is and how the part is laid out, then [Documents, pages and sites](wdoc_sites) for the shape of a build. After that, look up the block you need."

  p "Three conventions run through every chapter:"

  list {
    li "**Snippets were run.** A `.wcl` listing that carries a filename is a whole file, schema included, unless the surrounding text says it replaces part of one. A `$` listing is real terminal output, copied rather than typed out. The failures are as real as the successes."
    li "**Chapter links carry the chapter's name.** [Schemas](lang_schemas) goes to a chapter. [How WCL differs](#how-wcl-differs-from-json-yaml-and-toml) goes to a heading in this one. The build checks every link in this book, so a link that renders is a link that resolves."
    li "**Gaps are stated, not hidden.** Where the tool does not check something it describes, a warning callout says so. Where a feature is deliberately unfinished, the chapter names it rather than leaving you to find out."
  }
  h2 "Where to go next"

  list {
    li "[Quick start](quickstart). Install the tool and take one file from four lines to a checked schema. The next chapter."
    li "[Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents). Fields, blocks, labels, tables and `let` items in full. The start of Part I."
    li "[Values and primitives](lang_values). The seven ways to write a scalar, and which of them the checker judges."
    li "[Schemas](lang_schemas). `@document`, `@block`, `@child`, `@children`, `@inline`, `@table`, and exactly what `wcl check` checks."
    li "[Documents, pages and sites](wdoc_sites). The start of Part II, and the wdoc equivalent of [Documents, fields and blocks](lang_documents)."
  }
}
